# gitleaks ignore list
#
# Scope: exactly two findings, both on one line of one file, both synthetic.
#
#   commit e01dbb2, test/store/redaction.test.mjs:70
#     jwt-base64  — the base64 blob the fixture was stored as
#     jwt         — the same token, decoded from that blob
#
# The value is a JWT fixture for `sub: "1234567890"` with the signature
# `dBjftJeZ4CVP-mB92K27uhbUJU1p1r_wW1gFWFOEjXk`. It is the well-known example
# token used throughout JWT documentation, it carries no credential, and no
# service anywhere accepts it. The test it belongs to proves the opposite of a
# leak: that a secret-shaped value never reaches the store's bytes.
#
# WHY AN IGNORE FILE EXISTS AT ALL
#
# gitleaks scans commit history, and e01dbb2 is an ancestor of every later
# commit, so the finding will be reported on every scan forever. The alternative
# is rewriting published history, which is not something this project does
# without explicit permission.
#
# The blob is gone from the current tree: the fixture is now built from its
# segments at runtime, and a repository containing only the new shape reports
# `no leaks found`. That fix is in the commit that adds this file. What remains
# here is a record of a historical artefact, not a live exemption.
#
# This file is deliberately verbose and deliberately narrow. It names two exact
# fingerprints rather than a path or a rule, so:
#
#   - a real secret added to this file, or anywhere else, does not match it;
#   - the entries can only ever suppress these two findings on this one line;
#   - if the fixture line changes, the fingerprints stop matching and the finding
#     comes back — which is the correct failure, because then nobody has read it.
#
# Remove these two lines only together with the history that contains e01dbb2.

